---
import LessonLayout from '../../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
const lessonRef = "CCC 1499-1532";
const lessonScripture = "James 5:14-15; Mark 6:12-13; Isaiah 53:4-5; Matthew 8:17";
---
<LessonLayout title="Anointing of the Sick" cic={lessonRef} scripture={lessonScripture}>

  <section class="content">
    <h2>Big Question</h2>
    <p class="big-question">Where is God when the body aches and the soul falters?</p>
    <p>
      Imagine a man sitting in the dust, covered in sores from head to toe. He has lost everything: his children, his wealth, his health. His name is Job. In the midst of his pain, his friends try to explain his suffering as a punishment, but Job knows this isn't true. From the depths of his agony, he cries out something that has echoed for millennia: <em>"For I know that my Redeemer lives"</em> (Job 19:25). Suffering is the most difficult mystery of human life. It makes us feel alone, forgotten, even punished. But Jesus did not come to give us a theoretical explanation of pain; He came to suffer it with us and give it a new meaning. The Anointing of the Sick is precisely Christ's embrace telling us: "You are not alone in this battle; my strength is yours."
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content">
    <h2>Christ the Physician: Healer of Souls and Bodies</h2>
    <p>
      Jesus' entire life was a struggle against illness and suffering. St. Matthew recalls that Jesus "took our infirmities and bore our diseases" (Mt 8:17). When Jesus healed a paralytic or a blind man, He was not just seeking to alleviate physical pain; He was seeking to restore the whole person. His healings were signs that the Kingdom of God was arriving—a Kingdom where there will be no more weeping or pain (CCC 1503-1505).
    </p>

    <div class="depth-box depth-box--semilla">
      <h4 class="depth-box__title">Activity: Seed (Children)</h4>
      <strong>Jesus' First Aid Kit:</strong> Draw a doctor's bag. Inside, write three things Jesus uses to heal hearts (for example: love, forgiveness, peace). Did you know that the oil of Anointing is like "food" for the soul when the body is tired?
    </div>

    <p>
      Jesus did not heal every sick person in the world, but He showed a preferential compassion for them. By becoming man, He sanctified human suffering. Now, when we suffer, we do not do it alone: we are united to Christ on the Cross, collaborating in a mysterious but real way with the salvation of the world (CCC 1506).
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content">
    <h2>Institution of the Sacrament: From the Apostles to Us</h2>
    <p>
      Jesus sent His disciples to heal the sick: "They went out... and anointed with oil many that were sick and healed them" (Mark 6:12-13). But it is in the Letter of James where we find the definitive form of this sacrament for the Church. James asks: "Is any among you sick? Let him call for the elders of the church, and let them pray over him, anointing him with oil in the name of the Lord" (James 5:14). It is not a human invention or a "superstition"; it is a command from Christ entrusted to His apostles to care for the most fragile members of His Body (CCC 1510-1511).
    </p>

    <div class="depth-box depth-box--brotes">
      <h4 class="depth-box__title">Connection: Sprouts (Youth)</h4>
      <strong>Magic or Sacrament?:</strong> Read James 5:14-15. Notice that the text mentions three things: calling the elders (the Church), the prayer of faith, and oil. Anointing is not a magic charm that guarantees instant physical health, but an encounter with the grace that heals hopelessness. What is the difference between seeking a miracle and seeking God's grace?
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="content">
    <h2>Who Can Receive This Sacrament?</h2>
    <p>
      There is a false idea that this sacrament is only for those about to die (formerly called "Extreme Unction"). On the contrary! It is the sacrament of the <em>sick</em>. It can be received before a serious illness, before major surgery, or due to the frailty of old age. If the sick person recovers and becomes seriously ill again, they can receive it once more. The goal is to provide strength to the Christian when they begin to be in danger due to illness or old age (CCC 1514-1515).
    </p>

    <div class="depth-box depth-box--raiz">
      <h4 class="depth-box__title">Context: Root (New Adults)</h4>
      <strong>The Meaning of Anointing:</strong> Many families wait until the last breath to call the priest for fear of scaring the sick person. However, the Church teaches that Anointing should be received while lucid if possible, so that the person can participate in the peace and prayer of the sacrament. How can we change the vision from a "sacrament of death" to a "sacrament of life and hope"?
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="content">
    <h2>Effects of Anointing: Grace for the Battle</h2>
    <p>
      Illness can be a moment of profound crisis, where faith is put to the test. The effects of this sacrament are four pillars of hope:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Strength, peace, and courage:</strong> The Holy Spirit helps the sick person overcome the temptation of despair or anguish (CCC 1520).</li>
      <li><strong>Union with Christ's Passion:</strong> Suffering acquires a sacred value, becoming an offering for the Church (CCC 1521).</li>
      <li><strong>Forgiveness of sins:</strong> If the sick person has not been able to confess, Anointing forgives sins if there is repentance (CCC 1520).</li>
      <li><strong>Preparation for the final journey:</strong> If it is God's will to call the person home, the sacrament strengthens them for the final meeting (CCC 1523).</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="depth-box depth-box--arbol">
      <h4 class="depth-box__title">Deepening: Tree (Adult Formation)</h4>
      <strong>Viaticum:</strong> When a Christian is in danger of death, the Church offers them, in addition to Anointing, the Eucharist as "Viaticum" (food for the journey). It is the definitive passage from death to life, from this world to the Father. Reflect on the beauty of dying "fed" by the Body of Christ for the most important journey of existence (CCC 1524-1525).
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="content">
    <h2>Biblical Connection: The Good Samaritan</h2>
    <blockquote>
      <p>"But a Samaritan, as he journeyed, came to where he was; and when he saw him, he had compassion, and went to him and bound up his wounds, pouring on oil and wine; then he set him on his own beast and brought him to an inn, and took care of him." <cite>Luke 10:33-34</cite></p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      Jesus is the Good Samaritan of humanity. When we are wounded by the side of the road by illness, He does not pass by. He bends over us, cleanses our wounds with the holy oil of Anointing, and takes us to the "inn," which is the Church, to be cared for. The Church continues the Samaritan's mission today, offering the holy oil that consoles and the wine of grace that gladdens the heart (CCC 1503).
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content">
    <h2>For Reflection</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Have I ever seen someone receive Anointing? What peace or change did I notice in that person?</li>
      <li>Why is it so hard for us to see suffering as an opportunity to unite ourselves with Jesus?</li>
      <li>How can we help a sick relative desire this sacrament without fear?</li>
      <li>If Anointing does not always heal the body, what does it truly heal deep down?</li>
      <li>What does it mean that our pain can "help others" through Christ?</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="content">
    <h2>Key Idea</h2>
    <div class="callout">
      <p><strong>Key Idea:</strong> Anointing is not the sacrament of death, but Christ's embrace that strengthens life in the moment of weakness.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="content">
    <h2>Cultural Wealth</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>St. Camillus de Lellis:</strong> Known as the giant of charity, St. Camillus founded an order dedicated exclusively to the sick. He saw in every sufferer the face of the suffering Christ. His motto was "to put the heart in the hands" when touching a sick person, remembering that Christian charity is the best "anointing" before the sacrament.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The Sacrament of Mercy in Art:</strong> Various 17th-century oil paintings show the priest anointing the sick person surrounded by their family in prayer. These works highlight the communal dimension of the sacrament: the sick person does not suffer alone; the whole Church prays with and for them.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Film "Letters to Father Jacob":</strong> A beautiful Finnish film that shows how caring for others and prayer in the midst of physical frailty can redeem an entire life. It is a visual meditation on the grace that acts where human strength ends.
    </p>
  </section>

</LessonLayout>